<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frederick, Margrave of Brandenburg-Bayreuth</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1711">1711</a>–<a href="/years/1763">1763</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">House of Hohenzollern</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Frederick was Margrave of Brandenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayreuth">Bayreuth</a> from 1735 to 1763 and husband of Wilhelmine, sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick the Great</a>. Together they made Bayreuth a centre of art and music, building the Margravial Opera House, now a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>, and new palaces. In 1742 he founded a university at Bayreuth, moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erlangen">Erlangen</a> the next year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederick-margrave-of-brandenburg-bayreuth.jpg" alt="Frederick, Margrave of Brandenburg-Bayreuth" width="320" height="408" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_438_Friedrich_III_von_Brandenburg-Bayreuth.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Frederick was born at Weferlingen on 10 May 1711, the eldest son of George Frederick Karl, nominal Margrave of Brandenburg-Bayreuth-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kulmbach">Kulmbach</a>, and Dorothea of Schleswig-Holstein-Sonderburg-Beck, a minor branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">House of Hohenzollern</a>. His prospects changed in 1726, when his father inherited the principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayreuth">Bayreuth</a> after a long dispute with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> over the succession, and Frederick became Hereditary Margrave. He studied for eight years at the Calvinistic Genovese University, but his father and the ministers kept him out of government entirely, so he came to power unprepared.</p><p>On 20 November 1731 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> he married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelmine-of-bayreuth">Wilhelmine of Prussia</a>. He had been betrothed to her younger sister Sophie, but King Frederick Wilhelm I chose his eldest surviving daughter at the last moment without consulting the groom. The marriage went well from the start; their only child, Elisabeth Friederike Sophie, married Karl Eugen, Duke of Württemberg, in 1748.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Frederick succeeded his father in 1735. Wilhelmine tried to draw her husband, whom contemporaries saw as weak and unstable, towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, and in time secured the appointment of a young secretary, Philipp Elrodt, who acted almost as prime minister. Elrodt attacked corruption, uncovered irregularities in the finances, paid off old debts and found new income, which let Wilhelmine buy the summer residence of the Eremitage. Frederick also founded the Bayreuth Academy of Arts in 1756, enlarged the Eremitage with the Temple of the Sun (1749-1753) and built a new Margravial Castle after the old one burned. As Generalfeldmarschall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconian-circle">Franconian Circle</a> he kept his land out of the quarrels between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and Prussia, even in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>About a year after Wilhelmine died, he married Duchess Sophie Caroline Marie of Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfenbuttel">Wolfenbüttel</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a> on 20 September 1759; the marriage was childless. Frederick died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayreuth">Bayreuth</a> on 26 February 1763 and, having no son, was succeeded by his uncle Frederick Christian. He is remembered in Bayreuth as the Beloved, and the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erlangen">Erlangen</a> still bears his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Frederick, Margrave of Brandenburg-Bayreuth” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Margravine Wilhelmine, sister of Frederick the Great, and her husband <b>Frederick</b> made Bayreuth a cultural centre, building the Margravial Opera House and founding the university at Erlangen in 1743.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-brandenburg-bayreuth">Principality of Brandenburg-Bayreuth</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Weferlingen, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayreuth">Bayreuth</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.32,11.06&amp;year=1744" aria-label="Weferlingen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4798 358 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Frederick, Margrave of Brandenburg-Bayreuth"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5248 628h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5248 628h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.32,11.06&amp;year=1744">What was here in 1744? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
